#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348191 :question/problem "![](images/16/1617a8efd9110fa435dc396d90450c7fe17d18105e2358ab5610135e32a385ad.png)\n\nThe half-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-3,-1),$ $a=4$ and $b=2.$  Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a half-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the line parallel to the $x$-axis and crossing the center of the ellipse.\n\n![](images/46/4659fda20ed9a2b5e5c10d60f504e4db378efb0bfe9ae037f4998511e75300cb.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\pi ]$, we get the half-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the half-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348192 :question/problem "![](images/da/dafaf384e39d336444f11ed4acea1f586058c990253e434ae148cbddebbe0abd.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $8$ and vertical radius $5,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(8,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=8$ and $b=5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348193 :question/problem "![](images/c7/c7bb3c2ce6fe6fe2ea8c3a772b01077bb2fe4e40e005c05682989e8ea82ccc73.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(4,6)$ with horizontal radius $8$ and vertical radius $6,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(12,6)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(4,6),$ $a=8$ and $b=6.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +6,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348194 :question/problem "![](images/e5/e5d32d7b081238d3717a03f7718fcf8e3de8a7a00e5a04eada0129d1bcc43dc3.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(-2,5)$ with horizontal radius $6$ and vertical radius $4,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(4,5)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-2,5),$ $a=6$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348219 :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -5,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -5 \\\\ \\frac{x+5}{3} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2 \\\\ \\frac{y+2}{6} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{36} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{36} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{36}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351189 :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=\\sqrt{x}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(1,1),$ then  $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+y)\\,dx+(3x+2y)\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=\\sqrt{x}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) &=x(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}={t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+y)\\,dx+(3x+2y)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+(3x+2y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(({x}^{2}+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(3x+2y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({({t}^{2})}^{2}+t)\\cdot 2t+(3{t}^{2}+2t)\\cdot 1\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{4}+t)\\cdot 2t+3{t}^{2}+2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{t}^{5}+5{t}^{2}+2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{6}}{3}+\\frac{5{t}^{3}}{3}+{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{(1)^{6}}{3}+\\frac{5(1)^{3}}{3}+(1)^{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{5}{3}+1]-[0] \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351216 :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y={x}^{2}-x$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(2,2),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}2xy\\,dx+x\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y={x}^{2}-x.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) &=x(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+({t}^{2}-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}t\\in [0,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=\\mathbf{i}+(2t-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}2xy\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2t({t}^{2}-t)\\cdot 1+t(2t-1)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2{t}^{3}-t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{2}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =8-2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351218 :question/problem "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from the point $A(0,1)$ to the point $B(2,3),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}{e}^{x}\\,dx-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{2}\\right)\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{j}+t(2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{j}+t(2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(1+2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=2t$ and $y(t)=1+2t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x}\\,dx-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{2}\\right)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{2}\\right)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{x}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{2}\\right)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2t}\\cdot 2-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi (1+2t)}{2}\\right)\\cdot 2\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{e}^{2t}-2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi t))dt \\\\  & ={[{e}^{2t}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi t)]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[{e}^{2(1)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi (1))]-[{e}^{2(0)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi (0))] \\\\  & =[{e}^{2}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)]-[1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)] \\\\  & =[{e}^{2}+0]-[1+0] \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351250 :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y=\\pi {x}^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(1,\\pi )$ to point $B(2,4\\pi ),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)\\,dx+3x\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=\\pi {x}^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) &=x(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\pi {t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}t\\in [1,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=\\mathbf{i}+2\\pi t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)\\,dx+3x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+3x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+3x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi {t}^{2}}{2{t}^{2}}\\right)\\cdot 1+3t\\cdot 2\\pi t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+6\\pi {t}^{2}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}1+6\\pi {t}^{2}\\,dt \\\\  & =[t+2\\pi {t}^{3}]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[2+2\\pi (2)^{3}]-[1+2\\pi (1)^{3}] \\\\  & =[2+16\\pi ]-[1+2\\pi ] \\\\  & =1+14\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351267 :question/problem "![](images/6e/6e9d2238ca77532c3adda8d35f94061d108c3e15432ce48fdc33885ddf4f4e48.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(5,\\frac{7\\pi }{6})$ and $m∠AOB=\\frac{5\\pi }{6},$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(5,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}),$ we must have $r=5.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $5,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $5.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle clockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(5,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351268 :question/problem "![](images/a6/a615f7a80b81f89fccd8c82bf65d65e3a4a65958ec8a6fbc746ae68749949a87.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(10,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ and $m∠AOB=\\pi ,$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(10,\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ we must have $r=10.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $10,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $10.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle counterclockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\pi =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(10,\\frac{4\\pi }{3})$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =10\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =10\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-5\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-5,-5\\sqrt{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351278 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $$\n\\displaystyle T(\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, we have $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)$ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v).$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v) & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)\\, & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351299 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle x=t-1,\\,y={t}^{2}+2\n$$\nfrom $t=-1$ to $t=2$ ?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+2)=2t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\sqrt{{(1)}^{2}+{(2t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351300 :question/problem "![](images/12/120220c7c1a24c68c1a9282548efcf62c34fd110bd11911799a2a83409f257ab.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle x={t}^{3}+1,\\,y=3t\n$$\nfrom $t=-2$ to $t=0$ (shown above)?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+1)=3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\sqrt{(3{t}^{2})^{2}+(3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+1}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351310 :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields.\n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=2xy$ and $Q={x}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})$ and $Q=2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2}).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=y{e}^{xy}$ and $Q=x{e}^{xy}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $F(x,y)=2xy\\,i+{x}^{2}\\,j$; $G(x,y)$ $=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})\\,i$ $+2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})\\,j$; $H(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}\\,i+x{e}^{xy}\\,j$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351316 :question/problem "What is the period of the function $f(x)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x-{1}^{∘})?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $f(x)=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{{360}^{∘}}{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=9.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{{360}^{∘}}{9}={40}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351356 :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $\\mathrm{∇}f=\\mathbf{F},$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=(2x{y}^{2}+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2{x}^{2}y+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x{y}^{2}+1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{x}^{2}y+1\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2x{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{x}^{2}y+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{x}^{2}y+1$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}y+{g}^{′}(y) & =2{x}^{2}y+1 \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =1 \\\\ g(y) & =y+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=y+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x+y+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351357 :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y),$ where $$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=(12{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{y})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\left(\\frac{x-1}{{y}^{2}}\\right)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=12{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x-1}{{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (12{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{y})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{x}^{4}-\\frac{x}{y}+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x-1}{{y}^{2}}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}+{g}^{′}(y) & =\\frac{x-1}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}+{g}^{′}(y) & =\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ g(y) & =\\frac{1}{y}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=\\frac{1}{y}+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=3{x}^{4}-\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{1}{y}+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351358 :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where $F(x,y,z)=(y+1)\\,i+(x+z+1)\\,j+(y+1)\\,k.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y+1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x+z+1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=y+1\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (y+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =xy+x+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x+z+1$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =x+z+1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =z+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int (z+1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =yz+y+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=yz+y+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy+x+yz+y+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=y+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=y+1$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =y+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =1 \\\\ h(z) & =z+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy+yz+x+y+z+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351359 :question/problem "Consider the function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=y{e}^{xy}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x{e}^{xy}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3\\operatorname{cos}(3z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nFind $f(x,y,z)$ such that $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{\\text{i}}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{\\text{j}}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{\\text{k}}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y{e}^{xy},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{xy},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3z)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{xy}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{xy}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{xy}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =x{e}^{xy} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int 0\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={e}^{xy}+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3z)$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(z) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3z) \\\\ h(z) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3z)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={e}^{xy}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3z)+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351360 :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)$ and $Q=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $F$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=2x+1$ and $Q=5{y}^{2}+2y+1.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P={y}^{2}$ and $Q={x}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $H$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $G$ $=(2x+1)\\,i$ $+(5{y}^{2}+2y+1)\\,j$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351361 :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields.  \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=xy$ and $Q=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=4x+{y}^{2}$ and $Q=-4x{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-4{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $G$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)$ and $Q=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy),.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $H$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $F=xy\\,i+(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,j$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351362 :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $\\mathrm{∇}f=\\mathbf{F},$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}y+\\operatorname{sin}y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking  $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\n$$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{g}^{′}(y) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y \\\\ g(y) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351363 :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y),$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+(x{e}^{xy}+2y)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y{e}^{xy},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{xy}+2y\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{xy}+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{xy}+2y$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{xy}+{g}^{′}(y) & =x{e}^{xy}+2y \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =2y \\\\ g(y) & ={y}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)={y}^{2}+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={e}^{xy}+{y}^{2}+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351364 :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z),$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=y{z}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(x{z}^{2}+z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(2xyz+y-2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y{z}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{z}^{2}+z,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=2xyz+y-2\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int y{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =xy{z}^{2}+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{z}^{2}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{z}^{2}+z$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{z}^{2}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =x{z}^{2}+z \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =yz+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=yz+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy{z}^{2}+yz+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=2xyz+y+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=2xyz+y-2$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xyz+y-2 & =2xyz+y+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =-2 \\\\ h(z) & =-2z+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy{z}^{2}+yz-2z+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351365 :question/problem "Consider the function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=(yz{e}^{xz}+z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+({e}^{xz}+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(xy{e}^{xz}+x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nFind $f(x,y,z)$ such that $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{\\text{i}}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{\\text{j}}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{\\text{k}}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=yz{e}^{xz}+z,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={e}^{xz}+1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=xy{e}^{xz}+x\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (yz{e}^{xz}+z)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =y{e}^{xz}+xz+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={e}^{xz}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={e}^{xz}+1$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{xz}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & ={e}^{xz}+1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int 1\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =y+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=y+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=y{e}^{xz}+xz+y+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=xy{e}^{xz}+x+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=xy{e}^{xz}+x$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy{e}^{xz}+x+{h}^{′}(z) & =zy{e}^{xz}+x \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=y{e}^{xz}+xz+y+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351514 :question/problem "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}{x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}\\le 4,\\hspace{0.222em}x\\ge 0\\},$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/96/968419bad65410864c75e8abe766d21ac4d4acd835c73ea84118cf21ac066f79.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(0,2).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta -4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/2f/2fe33fb6e1be8b6107cb667e9453613af906bd4da7d67f9d9323d49cce5ae06c.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =0$ (the positive $x$-axis) and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (the positive $y$-axis):\n\n![](images/e2/e2b5e7b4433cbfb50ac7a9f991e4e4bc0cf8deecfefeeef0a4c9606ab6183cce.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le r\\le 4\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\sqrt{{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351516 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ({3}^{4}-{1}^{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =20{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate with respect to $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  & =20[\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }{2}]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =10{[{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =10({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}(0)) \\\\  & =10(1-0) \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351517 :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y{e}^{x}-xy$ increase most rapidly at the point $(-1,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}y+y{e}^{x}-xy)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}y+y{e}^{x}-xy)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}y+y{e}^{x}-y)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+(\\frac{x}{y}+{e}^{x}-x)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\ \\mathrm{∇}f(1,1) & =(\\operatorname{ln}1+1\\cdot {e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+(\\frac{-1}{1}+{e}^{-1}-(-1))\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =({e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{-1}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $({e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{-1}\\mathbf{j}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351520 :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)}{y}+\\pi xy.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{v}=⟨-2,2⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(1,1).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $\\mathbf{u}$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖\\mathbf{v}‖ & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}}=2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\mathbf{u} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇\\,g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)}{y}+\\pi xy),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)}{y}+\\pi xy)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)}{y}+\\pi y,\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)}{{y}^{2}}+\\pi x⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,g(1,1) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)}{1}+\\pi \\cdot 1,\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)}{{1}^{2}}+\\pi \\cdot 1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\pi +\\pi ,0+\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\pi ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(1,1) & =\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}g(1,1)\\cdot \\mathbf{u} \\\\  & =⟨0,\\pi ⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot (-1)+\\pi \\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351521 :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡y-2y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{v}=⟨-1,1⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}$ is equal to the directional derivative $$\n{g}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $\\mathbf{u}$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖\\mathbf{v}‖ & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\mathbf{u} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇\\,g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡y-2y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡y-2y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{tan}⁡y-2y{sec}^{2}⁡x,\\,2x{sec}^{2}⁡y-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,g(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨2\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right),\\,2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\cdot 1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 2,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2-\\pi ,\\,\\pi -2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative $$\n{g}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})\n$$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}g(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot \\mathbf{u} \\\\  & =⟨2-\\pi ,\\pi -2⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{(2-\\pi )\\cdot (-1)+(\\pi -2)\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi -4}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(\\pi -2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351551 :question/problem "![](images/95/959705e66be54eff155f5e784f5a42c8fb5098b9fdf9ec877b794f196bd674bf.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the fourth quadrant is enclosed between two quarter-circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $2,$ as shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le r\\le 2,\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}((r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351552 :question/problem "![](images/ce/ce35f6ef14576f536c268dd7420af6e0226609ab5305e85770f400439c2435de.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where the region $D$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ centered at $(\\frac{1}{2},0)\\,.$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $$\n\\displaystyle {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4},\n$$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}+{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )-r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$  So, the entire region lies between the radial lines $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/f5/f518a206638071a7b8548882a453123b6d78aed0aa94cc0e6e23429f22c6f1b3.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}\\theta \\le r\\le \\operatorname{cos}\\theta ,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\frac{2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sec}\\theta /2}^{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{sec}\\theta /2}^{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta [r]_{\\operatorname{sec}\\theta /2}^{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -1)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}(2\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{sin}(2\\theta )]_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354273 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{3\\pi /4}{\\int }_{2}^{4}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta =\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{3\\pi /4}{\\int }_{2}^{4}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{3\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2}^{4}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{3\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{2}^{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{3\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ({4}^{2}-{2}^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{3\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate with respect to $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{3\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =6[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]_{\\pi /6}^{3\\pi /4} \\\\  & =6(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)) \\\\  & =6(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =3(\\sqrt{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354274 :question/problem "Evaluate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{1}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1-\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }^{1}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\hspace{0.222em}[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]_{1-\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }^{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}({1}^{2}-(1-\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate with respect to $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  & ={[\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\frac{1}{4}\\theta -\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}(2\\theta )]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{8}-\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\frac{8-\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354276 :question/problem "Integrate the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}$ over the portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at the origin, where $x\\le 0$ and $y\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/e4/e4275e74a45deb22c0ed22546772e33fbd5caeb89a4036b1a2f4870e8ecf67a1.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{2}{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+1}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2r}{{r}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}+1)]_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2[\\theta ]_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354278 :question/problem "![](images/d0/d0d72be437a462f5eea781e83cc2a95e956e11ddc3295ad3b02a4a57867d9271.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where the region $D$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin.\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain $r=2.$\n\n- For $y=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-1 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-1 \\\\ r & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(\\sqrt{3},-1).$ So, the entire region lies between the radial lines $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/23/2386c1dcb1cf71231f2bb508dc1dd85dadef15b806eaf7f9a9ad0d3e4f3da1ca.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le -\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\frac{2r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{-\\pi /6}{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{csc}\\theta }^{2}2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{-\\pi /6}2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta {\\int }_{-\\operatorname{csc}\\theta }^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{-\\pi /6}2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta [r]_{-\\operatorname{csc}\\theta }^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{-\\pi /6}2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta (2+\\operatorname{csc}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{-\\pi /6}(4\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +2)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[-4\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +2\\theta ]_{-\\pi /2}^{-\\pi /6} \\\\  & =[(-4\\operatorname{cos}\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)-\\frac{\\pi }{3})-(0-\\pi )] \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354389 :question/problem "![](images/0d/0d91ac9127718b0f2755ec14c268d23af810c2ec6f13b4935930b4b348b76b34.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the plane is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $3,$ as shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{ }\\mathrm{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}({r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.167em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.167em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}2\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354390 :question/problem "![](images/15/15e58073c87bd249473950680a49e8fc4cb4be94277a7001c0ab9f96ab3a46df.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve $(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and the line $x=-3,$ as shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(-2,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +2)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +4r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +4+{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )+4r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+4r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+4\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $x=-3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-3 \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-3 \\\\ r & =-\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the points $(-3,-\\sqrt{3})$ and $(-3,\\sqrt{3}).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/92/928c6dc79f44a2185ccf217c0cf9210630b6554a698f805ef2c77500f9ddc76b.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{5\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}{\\int }_{-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{5\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354391 :question/problem "![](images/dc/dc35d0362ef95d11402ad44cded01abf46686d3088e9d852bd0b9a7ba2454e16.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the line $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},$ as shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(0,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ we obtain $r=1.$\n\n- For $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ r & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).\n$$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $\\theta =\\pi ,$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/ca/ca09f228fc218ef1b57196c6b6b1e740c10173b7cd38b1c27407726eccd783f1.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{2}}\\le r\\le 1,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}\\theta /\\sqrt{2}}^{1}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )}\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}\\theta /\\sqrt{2}}^{1}{r}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
